   ("WSM-OS-RUNTIMECONTEXT-EXCLUSIVE-OWNERSHIP" .
    ((priority . 7.8)
     (capabilities . (rust memory-safety unsafe soundness runtime))
     (origin . wsm-os-lisp)
     (description . "crates/wsm-os-runtime's RuntimeContext::new / new_with_closures are unsafe fn whose entire safety contract -- 'the arena stays exclusively owned for the context's lifetime' -- is caller-enforced prose, not typestate. Nothing in the type system stops a second RuntimeContext being constructed over the same heap pointer/arena, which would violate every bounds-check invariant the rest of the crate (cell()/closure_descriptor()'s address-offset/alignment/len checks) relies on. Every other unsafe block in this crate carries a specific, locally-verifiable SAFETY comment tied to a check performed nearby; this constructor contract is the one place that pattern breaks down. In the current single-threaded bare-metal setting this is probably benign, but the gap between what ADR-003/DEFINITION-CAPSULE claim about bounded, capability-checked memory and what the type system actually proves here is real. Either encode exclusive ownership in the type (a marker/token that can't be duplicated, or making the constructor genuinely safe by construction) or add an explicit doc-comment + regression test demonstrating why a second RuntimeContext over the same arena cannot occur in this codebase's actual call graph today.")
     (done . (t . "wsl-nidana-1 2026-09-01: audited call graph (exactly 4 construction sites: 2 self-contained test helpers in wsm-os-runtime, plus wsm-os-kernel's single entry_point! kernel_main and wsm-os-hosted's main, each building one private arena once). Chose the checked-guard path over typestate to avoid touching #[repr(C)]'s FFI-facing layout: added a per-arena (not global) live-pointer registry (claim_arena/release_arena, AtomicUsize slots, no_std/no-alloc) that panics on a genuine same-pointer double-construction but doesn't false-positive on distinct concurrent arenas (parallel tests, future multi-context hosts); wired into new()/new_with_closures(), released via a new Drop impl. Regression tests added: constructing_a_second_context_over_the_same_arena_panics, dropping_a_context_releases_its_arena_for_reuse. Verified the guard is load-bearing, not decorative: temporarily disabled the two claim_arena call sites and confirmed the panic test then correctly fails (no panic fires); restored and re-confirmed 7/7 wsm-os-runtime tests pass. Also confirmed both real consumers still build clean: cargo build -p wsm-os-hosted and -p wsm-os-kernel --target x86_64-unknown-none --release, plus a hosted run still prints the unchanged (A . B) fixture result."))))

   ("WSM-OS-PCI-CAPABILITY-UNFORGEABILITY-HARDENING" .
    ((priority . 8.3)
     (capabilities . (wsm rust capability-model pci security))
     (origin . wsm-os-lisp)
     (depends-on . (WSM-OS-WSM-PCI-CONFIG-CAPABILITY-D1))
     (description . "wsm_pci_config_capability's current 'unforgeable' guarantee is thinner than ADR-003's prose implies: a capability is just a tagged Word, and validity today means 'decodes correctly AND equals the one constant this function hands out' -- which works only because exactly one capability ID exists. The moment a second capability class is admitted (MMIO, DMA, interrupts -- all named as expected primitive classes in ADR-003 section 2), equality-against-one-constant stops being a meaningful unforgeability check on its own; something has to actually distinguish which capability a Word represents and verify provenance, not just tag-shape. Design and prove a capability model that stays sound with more than one live capability ID before the next capability class (MMIO or DMA) is admitted -- do not add MMIO/DMA/virtqueue logic in this task, this is scoped to the capability-identity mechanism itself.")
     (done . ())))

